        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಚಾಕು",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಚಾಕು (cāku) — \"a knife\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Persian chāqū, a small knife, carried down into the subcontinent along the same road that delivered the ಕುರ್ಚಿ, and handed on to Hindi, Marathi, Telugu and this. Two objects in two chapters, both borrowed, both arriving by the north."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The borrowing did not push anything out. Kannada's own blade is ಕತ್ತಿ (katti), and it is the big one — the household and field blade, the one a ರೈತ works with. ಚಾಕು (cāku) is the small one: the pocket knife, the paring knife, the thing that cuts a ಹಣ್ಣು."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The pattern is the one the chair showed. When a language borrows a word for a thing it already has, the borrowed word usually takes a narrower job and the old word keeps the wider one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three, and the second of them arrived by the northern road."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
