        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಪಾತ್ರೆ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಪಾತ್ರೆ (pātre) — \"a vessel, a cooking pot\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanskrit pātra, and it began by meaning a drinking or eating vessel. Then it did something quietly remarkable: it moved from the container to the person. A pātra is also one fit to receive — a worthy recipient of a gift, of teaching, of trust."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From there it took one more step, and the pātra of a play is a character, a part, a role. The same word holds the pot, the deserving person and the actor's part, because all three are shapes that something is poured into."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada carries the whole set. ಪಾತ್ರೆ (pātre) in the kitchen is the vessel; ಪಾತ್ರ on the stage is the role. One Sanskrit word, and the ending tells you which of its lives is being lived."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four, and this one keeps three meanings in one shape."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
