        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಪೆಟ್ಟಿಗೆ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಪೆಟ್ಟಿಗೆ (peṭṭige) — \"a box\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "All four sisters have this word and it is nearly the same in each: Tamil peṭṭi, Malayalam peṭṭi, Telugu peṭṭe, and Kannada ಪೆಟ್ಟಿಗೆ (peṭṭige). Only Kannada carries the extra closing syllable, which is a habit you have now seen a few times — Kannada tends to be the long one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A ಪೆಟ್ಟಿಗೆ (peṭṭige) is the wooden or tin chest a household keeps its papers and its good ಬಟ್ಟೆ in, standing in a corner with a padlock on it. It is also the word that stretched to cover a suitcase, a jewellery box and the case a musical instrument travels in."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Notice how much of this chapter is containers. The ಬುಟ್ಟಿ (buṭṭi) carries, the ಪಾತ್ರೆ (pātre) cooks, the ಪೆಟ್ಟಿಗೆ (peṭṭige) keeps. A house is largely a set of things for putting other things into."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five: ಚಾಪೆ (cāpe), ಬುಟ್ಟಿ (buṭṭi), ಚಾಕು (cāku), ಪಾತ್ರೆ (pātre), ಪೆಟ್ಟಿಗೆ (peṭṭige). You can furnish a room."
            }
          ]
        },
